// @run
// @log 3
// Regression (silent-class-inventory D1008), graduated from
// `error-u8-struct-field-unsupported.vl` — the file that kept this refusal LOUD while it
// was still a refusal:
//
//   emitProgram: struct field type `u8[]` has no struct-field rep
//
// The packed byte list shipped at every VALUE position — local, parameter, return, binding,
// global, closure capture — and at NO container position. A struct field was the one that
// mattered: `type Cur = { buf: u8[], pos: i32 }` is the VLB decoder's own cursor shape, and
// without it the decoder had to thread a pair of locals by hand or spell the buffer
// `string`. That is a workaround for a missing field code, not a design.
//
// Making it loud was right, and the hazard it guarded is real: without the refusal a `u8[]`
// field took `fieldCodeOfSpelling`'s `return 5` (a REF list) and the emitter interned a
// ref-list wrapper for a one-byte backing — check-clean invalid wasm. Leaving it loud was
// the clause-2 violation, since `vl check` accepts the program and the design permits it.
//
// CODE 35, NOT A SIXTH `scalarListKindOfName` ENTRY. That function's five codes are read by
// the map-value, field, closure-result and `$fnsig` param/result ladders, and a sixth entry
// would be a silent wrong answer in each of them until every one grew an arm. A code of its
// own is invisible to all four and wired only where it is handled — which is why this change
// closes the struct field without touching the other three container positions, and they
// keep refusing loudly (`error-u8-not-generic.vl`, `error-u8-storage-only.vl`).
type S = { b: u8[] }
const s: S = { b: [3] }
print(s.b[0])
